items/thm-monotone-one-sided-limits-exist.md:107:3.2 Given real $\varepsilon > 0$, the epsilon characterisation of the infimum gives $x_1 \in I$ with $x_1 > c$ and $f(x_1) < M + \varepsilon$; put $\delta := x_1 - c > 0$. For $x \in I$ with $c < x < c + \delta$ we have $c < x < x_1$, so $M \le f(x) \le f(x_1) < M + \varepsilon$ and hence $|f(x) - M| < \varepsilon$. [step 2.3, A1, L2]
items/thm-monotone-one-sided-limits-exist.md:119:- **Where completeness is spent.** Exactly once on each side, in the existence of $\sup S^{-}$ and of $\inf S^{+}$; the rest of the proof is the definition of a one-sided limit and the monotonicity hypothesis. Over an ordered field that is not complete the statement fails, because the supremum need not exist.
items/thm-monotone-one-sided-limits-exist.md:121:- **The two one-sided limits need not agree, and that is the point.** When both are defined they satisfy $\lim_{x \to c^{-}} f(x) \le f(c) \le \lim_{x \to c^{+}} f(x)$, and a strict inequality between the outer two is exactly a jump discontinuity; [[thm-monotone-discontinuities-are-jumps]] turns that observation into the classification of the discontinuities of a monotone function, and [[thm-froda]] counts them.
